## Where you write

Write only inside this project: the folder the session started in and everything under it,
worktrees under `.claude/worktrees/` included. Outside it, create, change, move or delete nothing
unless the user has explicitly allowed that path or action in this conversation. That rules out
your home folder, its dotfiles and tool config (`~/.config`, `~/.claude`, `~/.gitconfig`,
`~/.npmrc`), `/tmp` and every other temp folder (a scratchpad your tool offers included), other
repositories and projects, and system folders, and it rules out installing anything globally
(`npm install -g`, `pip install --user`, `cargo install`, `brew install`).

- It holds for every tool: Write and Edit, and every command — redirections (`>`, `>>`), `cp`,
  `mv`, `tee`, `ln`, `mkdir`, `touch`, output options (`-o`, `--output`, `--out-dir`), `git clone`
  or `git worktree add` into another folder, `git config --global`.
- Scratch files go in this project's `.claude/scratch/`, never in a temp folder. If it isn't there,
  create it with a `.gitignore` holding the one line `*`, and delete your files there as soon as
  they're no longer needed.
- Building, testing and linting the project with its usual commands is fine, though the tools keep
  their own caches outside it (cargo, npm, pip). Never point their output, an install or their
  config anywhere outside the project.
- When the work needs a write outside the project, stop and ask the user first: say exactly what
  would be written, and where. An approval covers only what the user named, for this task.
- When you hand work to an agent that doesn't get these instructions (a built-in one such as
  Explore, Plan or general-purpose), put this section in its prompt.
